{"title":"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 8","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/8/a28f330a30091315e4652d36a5865a65c8f5dcb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 101$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />, which of the following expressions has an even value?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/7/c672c527bacf0d745a421238dd502b61b413594c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 3n \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ n + 2 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ n - 12 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 2n - 2 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 3n + 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"522\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can find the value of each answer choice until we get one that is even.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/d/50d6af74b2d07bc3c05cd197d825d0dd6a41bc13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3n = 3 \\times 101 = 303$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"156\" height=\"15\" />. However, this is <i>not</i> even, so we can eliminate answer choice A.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/b/55bd1fe3a5a3815450c25f6d17c706a3a87386b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 2 = 101 + 2 = 103$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"178\" height=\"15\" />, which is <i>not</i> even. Thus, we can eliminate answer choice B.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/3/f33252960871c02f38f82aefe55c0d139bb8e9d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - 12 = 101 - 12 = 89$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"187\" height=\"15\" />, which is <i>not</i> even. Thus, we can eliminate answer choice C.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/7/5d79cdb5ae95fb4ac25fd6a6b71aed84827c9f09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n - 2 = 2 \\times 101 - 2 = 202 - 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"250\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/2/9121272314635eaf73c99c072531ae89f06c3c92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$=200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>This is an even number. Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/7/d979ce723b5f0999780c82773ecd390720e6856a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf {(D) } 2n - 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"100\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Without_Finding_Values.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Without_Finding_Values)\">Solution 2 (Without Finding Values)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Without Finding Values)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can go through each answer choice until we get one that is even.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/5/a45e3d817886b4d9dbd6bb74ad23463ed139ed68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> is an odd integer because both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/8/a28f330a30091315e4652d36a5865a65c8f5dcb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 101$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are odd. This means that we can eliminate answer choice A.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/b/b3be44477b1c4728ab06d90fde978271a2a909a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/7/ce7746e990820404412e7bf085abac912f75d9a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> are both odd because they involve either adding or subtracting an even number from an odd integer. Thus, we can eliminate answer choices B and C.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> is even because an even number multiplied by another number is always even. Subtracting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> from an even number results in an even number, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/7/d979ce723b5f0999780c82773ecd390720e6856a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf {(D) } 2n - 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"100\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 9\">Problem 9</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_7\" titl